::SPEECH_U03_L07_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U03_L07_GuidedPractice_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.01
Question:
Which choice best completes a persuasive speech plan?
{
~A. Use a louder voice so the audience agrees#This confuses delivery volume with persuasive planning. A strong speech still needs a clear claim, evidence, reasoning, and audience awareness.
=B. State a clear claim, support it with evidence, explain the reasoning, and answer a fair counterclaim#Correct. This answer directly supports the lesson skill by using the standard to make communication clearer, better supported, and more audience aware.
~C. Choose details because they sound interesting#Interesting details are not enough. Evidence and examples must connect directly to the claim, purpose, audience, or rebuttal.
~D. Make a personal attack against the opposing speaker#Personal attacks weaken credibility. Persuasive speakers should respond to ideas with evidence and respectful reasoning.
}
::SPEECH_U03_L07_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U03_L07_GuidedPractice_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.04
Question:
Which choice best connects evidence to reasoning?
{
~A. Use a louder voice so the audience agrees#This confuses delivery volume with persuasive planning. A strong speech still needs a clear claim, evidence, reasoning, and audience awareness.
~B. Ignore the opposing view and repeat the claim#This avoids comparison and weakens credibility. Persuasive communication should address opposing views respectfully and explain reasoning.
=C. Explain how the evidence proves the claim for the audience#Correct. This answer directly supports the lesson skill by using the standard to make communication clearer, better supported, and more audience aware.
~D. Make a personal attack against the opposing speaker#Personal attacks weaken credibility. Persuasive speakers should respond to ideas with evidence and respectful reasoning.
}
::SPEECH_U03_L07_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U03_L07_GuidedPractice_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.03
Question:
Which choice best handles an opposing argument?
{
=A. Name the opposing view respectfully and answer it with evidence-based reasoning#Correct. This answer directly supports the lesson skill by using the standard to make communication clearer, better supported, and more audience aware.
~B. Ignore the opposing view and repeat the claim#This avoids comparison and weakens credibility. Persuasive communication should address opposing views respectfully and explain reasoning.
~C. Choose details because they sound interesting#Interesting details are not enough. Evidence and examples must connect directly to the claim, purpose, audience, or rebuttal.
~D. Make a personal attack against the opposing speaker#Personal attacks weaken credibility. Persuasive speakers should respond to ideas with evidence and respectful reasoning.
}
::SPEECH_U03_L07_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U03_L07_GuidedPractice_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.02
Question:
Which choice best shows audience awareness?
{
~A. Use a louder voice so the audience agrees#This confuses delivery volume with persuasive planning. A strong speech still needs a clear claim, evidence, reasoning, and audience awareness.
~B. Ignore the opposing view and repeat the claim#This avoids comparison and weakens credibility. Persuasive communication should address opposing views respectfully and explain reasoning.
~C. Choose details because they sound interesting#Interesting details are not enough. Evidence and examples must connect directly to the claim, purpose, audience, or rebuttal.
=D. Choose tone and examples that fit what the audience needs to understand#Correct. This answer directly supports the lesson skill by using the standard to make communication clearer, better supported, and more audience aware.
}
